+#+TITLE: lazy-jail: spec report
+
+Gap analysis of =src/lazy-jail/spec.org= against the working copy. It
+is refreshed whenever the implementation changes.
+
+* Requirements
+
+Numbered here so the sections below can cite them. Lines are lines of
+=spec.org=. Lines 3-9 direct this report, and lines 32-40 hold the
+author's open questions; neither is a requirement.
+
+- R1 (line 12): a CLI utility for managing jails from source trees
+- R2 (lines 13-14): the jail config is checked in, giving a dev jail
+  for a repo
+- R3 (lines 15-16): the jail config is not centralized
+- R4 (lines 17-18): jails live in a known location, e.g.
+  =zroot/lj/instances=
+- R5 (line 20): =lj start <jail>=
+- R6 (line 21): =lj stop <jail>=
+- R7 (lines 22-24): =lj login <jail> [user]=, default user from the
+  config file
+- R8 (line 25): =lj list= lists running jails
+- R9 (lines 28-30): jail =meta= subkeys track lj jails, queryable from
+  any directory
+
+* Status
+
+Nothing in the spec is implemented yet. There are no conflicts and no
+conflict plans. None of the requirements is ready to implement. The
+spec names the four commands and the =meta= idea, but it does not say
+what a jail config contains, where it lives in a source tree, or what
+=start= and =stop= do to a jail's dataset. Its own closing question
+(lines 32-40), whether =lj= should exist as its own tool or be a thin
+layer over =jexec= and =service jail=, decides most of the others and
+should be settled first.
+
+* Conflicts
+
+None. Nothing implements the spec, so nothing can contradict it.
+
+=jails/instances= is a related design, but it is not an
+implementation of this spec, and lines 4-9 say not to treat it as one.
+Its differences from the spec are used below only as evidence for open
+questions.
+
+* Conflict plans that can be removed
+
+None. The spec has no =conflict plan:= headings:
+
+#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
+$ grep -c 'conflict plan:' src/lazy-jail/spec.org
+0
+#+END_EXAMPLE
+
+* Not implemented
+
+Evidence that nothing implements R1-R9: =src/lazy-jail/= holds only
+=spec.org=, and nothing else in the tree names =lj=, sets a jail
+=meta= key, or uses =zroot/lj=. A search for =\blj\b=, =meta\.=,
+=meta== and =zroot/lj= outside =.jj= and =dotfiles/nvim= matches only
+=spec.org= itself. The history of =src/lazy-jail= is a single commit,
+9de42dab "lazy-jail: Initial spec".
+
+All nine requirements are *not ready to implement*. The questions are
+grouped by the requirements they block. Several groups depend on Q1.
+
+** Q1: should =lj= exist as its own tool? (R1, R5-R8)
+
+The spec asks this itself, at lines 32-40: keep the =jails/instances=
+approach and rely on =jexec=, or make =lj= a thin layer over =jexec=
+and =service jail=. The answer decides what =start=, =stop= and
+=login= mean, so it has to come first.
+
+- If =lj= is a thin layer, does it drive =service jail= (which needs
+  the config installed in =/etc/jail.conf.d=, as
+  =jails/libexec/jail.subr:5-8= and =:18-20= do) or does it call
+  =jail -c -f <config>= straight from the source tree? Lines 15-16
+  ("not centralized") point to the second, and lines 36-37 ("=service
+  jail= commands") point to the first.
+
+** Q2: the config file (R2, R3, R7)
+
+The spec says the config is checked in to a repo (lines 13-14) and
+holds a default user (lines 23-24). It does not say anything else
+about it.
+
+- What is the file's name, and where does it sit in a source tree?
+  Can one repo define more than one jail?
+- What format is it? =jails/instances= uses two files: jail(8)
+  parameters in =jail.conf= (=jails/instances/lab-devel/jail.conf:1-12=)
+  and sh variables for the default user, users and packages in
+  =instance.conf= (=jails/instances/lab-devel/instance.conf:1-12=). Is
+  the lj config =jail.conf(5)= syntax, sh, or something else?
+- Which settings does it carry beyond the default user: users with
+  uid/gid, packages, pkgbase sets, files to install into the jail?
+  =jails/instances= supports all of these
+  (=jails/libexec/_jail.log.do:26-29=, =:112-180=).
+- If the config does not name a default user, does =lj login= fall
+  back to root, as =jails/libexec/jail.sh:15= does, or fail?
+
+** Q3: naming and lookup (R5-R9)
+
+- Is =<jail>= a jail name, a path to a source tree, or a path to a
+  config file? =jails/instances= derives the name from the current
+  directory (=jails/libexec/jail.subr:1=); the spec instead takes it
+  as an argument.
+- Must =lj start= run from inside the source tree, or can it find the
+  config from anywhere? Lines 28-30 say the other commands should work
+  from any directory; the spec does not say the same for =start=.
+- What happens when two repos define jails with the same name?
+
+** Q4: the source tree inside the jail (R1, R2)
+
+"A dev jail for a given repo" (lines 13-14) suggests the repo is
+reachable inside the jail, but the spec does not say how.
+
+- Is the source tree mounted into the jail (nullfs, read-write or
+  read-only), cloned into it, or not provided at all?
+- At what path inside the jail? =jails/instances= mounts shared git
+  repos at =${path}/data/= (=jails/instances/lab-devel/jail.conf:7-11=),
+  and its clone step is still a stub
+  (=jails/libexec/_jail.log.do:182-186=).
+
+** Q5: the known location and the jail's lifecycle (R4, R5, R6)
+
+Lines 17-18 give =zroot/lj/instances= only as an example ("e.g.").
+
+- Is the dataset fixed, or set by the operator (pool, parent dataset,
+  mountpoint)? =jails/instances= hard-codes =zroot/jails/instances=
+  (=jails/libexec/jail.subr:2-3=) and the path
+  =/jails/instances/<name>= in each =jail.conf=.
+- Does =lj= create the parent dataset, or fail when it is missing as
+  =jails/libexec/_jail.log.do:47-52= does?
+- Does =lj start= build the jail root (pkgbase, users, packages) when
+  it does not exist? =jails/instances= keeps building (=just build=,
+  =jails/share/_Justfile:4-5=) separate from starting (=just start=,
+  =:13-14=).
+- When the checked-in config changes, does =lj start= rebuild or
+  update the jail, or use what is already there?
+- Does =lj stop= only stop the jail, or also destroy its dataset? The
+  "lazy" in the name could mean either a jail kept between runs or one
+  made on demand. Is there a command to destroy a jail, like
+  =jails/libexec/_clean.do:16-18=?
+- How does =lj= get privilege: is it run as root, or does it call
+  =doas= the way =jails/libexec/jail.subr:19-29= does?
+
+** Q6: =lj list= (R8)
+
+- Does it list every running jail, or only the ones =lj= manages? If
+  only those, it depends on the =meta= key in Q7 to tell them apart.
+- What does it print for each jail: name, source tree, path, default
+  user?
+
+** Q7: =meta= keys (R9)
+
+The host supports the parameter: on FreeBSD 15.1-RELEASE-p4 =jls -n=
+lists both =meta= and =env=, and jail(8) describes them as sets of
+=key=value= strings settable as =meta.keyname=value=.
+
+- Which keys does =lj= write: source tree path, config path, default
+  user, a marker that =lj= manages the jail?
+- Lines 23-24 take the default user from the config file, but lines
+  28-30 want commands to work outside the source tree. Should
+  =lj login= read the default user from =meta= instead?
+- =meta= or =env=? jail(8) says =meta= is hidden from inside the jail,
+  and =env= is readable there through =security.jail.env=.
+
+** Q8: packaging (R1)
+
+- What is the tool written in, and how is =lj= installed on a host?
+  The directory is =src/lazy-jail= and the command is =lj=; is that
+  the intended pairing?
+
+* Implemented
+
+None.
